  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Max and Ilya are talking about a very impressive woman they know. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В смысле?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "В прямом смысле.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она умная и красивая.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я согласен с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она закончила университет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она изучала управление ресторанами.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень престижное образование.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "А потом она здесь работала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Она работала в нашем ресторане?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Да, она была менеджером.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не знал об этом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она очень талантливая девушка.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В смысле?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In what sense?' There is no question word here — in Russian, rising intonation on 'sense' is enough to make it a question. And Ilya jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "В прямом смысле.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In the direct sense.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'straight' with 'sense'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она умная и красивая.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She is smart and beautiful.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'it' directly with 'pretty'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я согласен с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I agree with you.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она закончила университет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She finished the university.' Ilya links 'it' directly with 'finished' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она изучала управление ресторанами.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She studied restaurant management.' Ilya combines 'it' with 'restaurant' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень престижное образование.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is a very prestigious education.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'this' with 'education'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "А потом она здесь работала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And then she worked here.' Notice how the verb meaning 'worked' takes the feminine past-tense ending '-a' here.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Она работала в нашем ресторане?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She worked in our restaurant?' Max combines 'it' with 'restaurant' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Ilya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Да, она была менеджером.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, she was a manager.' Ilya links 'yes' directly with 'it' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не знал об этом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I did not know about this.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'that'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ilya",
      "t": "Она очень талантливая девушка.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She is a very talented girl.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'it' straight to 'girlfriend'. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
